summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up of chronic problems.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Carolyn Williams is a 49-year-old female who is here for follow-up of her chronic problems.

The patient is doing okay. She reports that her osteoarthritis has been flaring up a little bit lately. It is primarily in her elbows and it has been bothering her. She has been sitting at a desk more at the office so she does not know if it is being stagnant in that position all day that has increased flare-ups for that, but she wanted to talk to me about that.

She is right-handed. She reports that her right elbow bothers her more than the other, but primarily she is typing all day versus writing, so it flares up in both. She denies any other joint pain. She reports that she sometimes feels a little bit of numbing in her hands when she is typing for long periods of time. She tries to shake out her arms a little bit to kind of relieve that sensation.

She has had this history of gout. The last episode was about 3 months ago. She had some inflammation of her toe. She has not had any other issues with that since the medication that I gave her really controlled it.

She reports that her psoriasis has been under control. She adds that the medication that I gave her for her scalp is doing a lot better.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

• Musculoskeletal: Reports osteoarthritis in the elbows, right-handed.
• Neurological: Endorses occasional numbness in the hands.

PHYSICAL EXAM

• Constitutional: In no acute distress.
• Skin: No rashes or lesions observed.
• HEENT: No cervical lymphadenopathy.
• Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheezes, rales, or rhonchi.
• Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, gallops, or rubs.
• Gastrointestinal: Abdomen is soft and non-tender. No hepatosplenomegaly.
• Musculoskeletal: Examination of the right elbow: Edema and inflammation of the right olecranon. Tenderness to palpation of the olecranon bursa. Pain with pronation and supination. Pain with flexion and extension of the right arm.
• Neurological: No lower extremity edema.

RESULTS

Autoimmune panel: WNL.
ESR: WNL.
Uric acid: WNL.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

The patient is a 49-year-old female with a history of osteoarthritis, gout, and psoriasis.

Osteoarthritis.
• Medical Reasoning: She has been having some flare-ups in her elbows. She has some edema and inflammation in her right elbow.
• Additional Testing: I would like to order an x-ray of her right elbow.
• Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe meloxicam 15 mg once a day for the pain and swelling. I am also going to prescribe colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups.

Gout.
• Medical Reasoning: Her last flare was 3 months ago and she is not having any other issues with that.
• Medical Treatment: Continue with colchicine 0.6 mg twice a day as needed for flare-ups.

Psoriasis.
• Medical Reasoning: Everything is stable at this time.
• Medical Treatment: Continue with steroid cream for her scalp.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
