Back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mrs. Lee is a 40-year-old female who presents today for the evaluation of back pain. The onset of her pain began when she was twisting at a birthday party. She states she was surprised and jumped up, fell down the stairs, and rammed her back into the railing. She denies any pain shooting down her legs. She also denies any history of back problems before her lumbar fusion. She states that the pain is worse when she lies flat and with twisting motions. She also reports that she has a history of anxiety and is taking medication for it.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Examination of the back shows no evidence of a pinched nerve or radiculopathy. Pain with rotation. No pain with leg lift.

RESULTS

X-rays of the lumbar spine obtained today show no evidence of compression fractures or significant abnormalities, except for the previous lumbar fusion.

ASSESSMENT

Strain of the back.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. I have recommended that she continue to take Motrin 800 mg 3 times a day with food. She is to use ice and heat alternating on her back, and I want her to walk. She will follow up with me over the next week if her symptoms do not improve or if they worsen. All questions were answered.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me over the next week if her symptoms do not improve or if they worsen. }}<|eot_id|>
