summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Follow-up for adult hydrocephalus.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Mr. Christopher Watson is a pleasant and cooperative 86-year-old male who returns in follow-up for adult hydrocephalus. He has been followed by me since 05/2020 when he first presented with 8 to 10 years of progressive gait impairment, cognitive impairment, and decreased bladder control. We established a diagnosis of adult hydrocephalus with a shunt protocol in 06/2020, and he underwent a Medtronic Strata programmable shunt in the ventricular peritoneal configuration programmed at a level 2.0, which was placed on 08/01/2020.

He reports that he has not been hospitalized since his shunt surgery 3 months ago. Before surgery, he was having progressive symptoms that affected his walking, thinking, and bladder control. These symptoms are improved. He states that his walking has improved and he is now able to walk to the dining room without assistance, whereas he was unable to do so prior to surgery. He also reports that his bladder control improved some after surgery but has leveled out, and he now wears a diaper. He denies any issues with sores from wearing the diaper and denies decreased or painful urination. He states that his cognitive impairment is improved, and he no longer feels lost when speaking with someone. He reports no headaches or pain at the shunt site. He also denies any pain in his abdomen.

PAST HISTORY

Medical
Hydrocephalus

CURRENT MEDICATIONS

Shunt.

PHYSICAL EXAM

CV
Blood pressure is 124/80, heart rate 64, respirations 18, weight 174 lbs, and pain 0 out of 10.

Neurological
Gait testing using the Tinetti assessment tool. He was tested without an assistive device and received a gait score of 6 to 8 out of 12 and a balance score of 12 out of 16 for a total score of 18 to 20 out of 28, which is slightly improved from his initial score of 15 to 17 out of 28.
Cranial nerves: Pupils are equal. EOMs are intact. Face symmetric. No dysarthria. Motor normal for bulk and strength. Coordination slow from no-finger to nose.

RESULTS

CT scan from 10/15/2020 of the head shows a frontal horn span at the level of the foramen of Monro of 4.6 cm with a third ventricular contour that is flat with a span of 10 mm. By my reading, there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. This was not noticed or noted by the radiologist who stated any extra-axial fluid collections. There is also substantial small vessel ischemic change.

ASSESSMENT

Adult hydrocephalus, gait impairment, urinary incontinence and urgency, and cognitive impairment.

PLAN

I reviewed the patient's history and current status. He is doing well. His symptoms are improved. I reviewed his CT scan from 10/15/2020, and there is a tiny amount of blood in the right frontal region with a tiny subdermal collection. I do not believe this is causing any symptoms and it was not noticed by the radiologist. I will continue to follow him. I will see him again in 3 months.

INSTRUCTIONS

We will schedule an appointment for 3 months from now. }}<|eot_id|>
