summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Rash on the face.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Jacqueline Miller is a 60-year-old female who presents for a follow-up visit for her perioral dermatitis.

The patient reports that she is doing well. She has a small bit of leftover rash. She started using doxycycline 100 mg once a day because she thinks the pharmacist said to limit it to once a day if it is getting better. She notes that she was taking it twice a day and she did notice a lot of improvement. She then started doing it only once a day and it seems the same and it's not continuing to get better. She is not breastfeeding at this time. She stopped breastfeeding and then asked for the doxycycline at that time. The patient did not take the Elidel because when she read about it, she got worried. She wanted to just try the doxycycline and see. The doctor explained that a lot of the things she read about is not actually relevant to the cream or ointment form, but it is about the oral form that is used in really high doses, long-term, after people have a heart transplant or a kidney transplant or something like that. The patient can resume taking the doxycycline twice a day again.

The patient reports that she is using the sulfacetamide wash twice a day. She is not drying out. She is using the Metrocream after her face wash. She is not using Cetaphil cleanser or Neutrogena wipes. She is not using any lotion right now, just those two. The patient went down to just taking doxycycline once a day last week. The first two weeks, she did twice a day and then she started seeing improvement and then she changed to just once a day.

The patient is Fitzpatrick skin type IV to V. On bilateral medial cheeks, there are a few really faint erythematous papules and just a little bit of redness around and underneath the nostrils. She is right, it is not totally gone.

The doctor would go ahead and go back to twice a day, every day, with the doxycycline.

The patient was advised that out of known risks associated with medications, topical are usually safer and preferable to oral medications. The doctor explained that doxycycline can give you bad upset stomach or heartburn. It will make you sunburn even if you never sunburn, so you have to protect yourself.

The patient was advised that if insurance will cover the Elidel, to pick it up and start using it. The patient did check and insurance is not covering it. The doctor stated that it is actually more expensive. She looked at the Eucrisa that the doctor recommended in her notes, and if it is still expensive, she could try that instead. The doctor stated that it is actually more expensive. It is actually more expensive. The prices fluctuate. When she looked before, it was around 30 to $40. When she looked now, it was around $70. The patient would have picked it up if it was around $200, but it was coming to around $850 after insurance. The doctor found the cream form. We could try ointment form.

The patient was advised that Elidel is not a steroid. It is called a calcineurin inhibitor. It is kinda like a steroid in that it calms inflammation. The doctor stated that it is not a steroid, so do not use steroids on the face, for sure, as they will make this kind of rash worse. Also, steroids carry the risk of causing thinning of the skin. These medications do not cause thinning of the skin and they are not going to cause some other kind of rash. The thing to know is that sometimes 5 to 10 minutes after you put it on, it can cause this weird kind of tingly or needle-like sensation or make it redder or flush. But it should only last a couple of seconds and then go away. It is not an allergy or anything bad. It does not mean it is going to happen again. It will still work, so keep using it as long as you know you can stand it, okay?

The patient was advised that once the rash stops, she can stop the tacrolimus ointment. Continue doxycycline another week and then stop. She can keep using the face wash and the Metrocream.

The patient was advised to hold off until she sees her again before she starts back any of her old products. If it is starting to flair again, if it is just a little bit, she can try just doing the tacrolimus for a couple of days to see if that is enough. If it is not, then restart the doxycycline as well.

The patient was advised that she will be seeing me for a follow-up. We can figure out what to do next. She will be seeing me for a follow-up. We can figure out what to do next. I would say if it is getting worse rather than better, just give me a call or send me a MyChart message, okay?

The patient was advised that she has another refill of the doxycycline, so she is good there. Metrocream, she probably still has enough of that one.

The patient was advised that we will get rid of the Clindamycin from her list as well. She has plenty of the face wash still.

The patient was advised that for that I would do something really basic like Vanicream. Or even if it is just really dry, you could use a little bit of petroleum jelly. I will give her a couple of samples, okay? Let's make sure we have a follow-up in about four weeks.

The patient was advised that I would stay away from any kind of anti-aging thing, any kind of plant thing. Really let's just be sure we talk about any new products, or reach out to me if she is going to try something else. Let's go get those samples.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Perioral dermatitis.

Patient Instructions

I recommend that she gently wash her eyelids once a day with a gentle cleanser such as Vanicream. Also use Bruder mask as needed.

INSTRUCTIONS

Continue using the same face wash, continue with Metrocream, and increase the use of doxycycline to twice a day. Once the rash stops, she can stop the tacrolimus ointment. Continue doxycycline another week and then stop. She can keep using the face wash and the Metrocream. }}<|eot_id|>
