Blood pressure, pulse, temperature, and respiratory rate were all within normal limits.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK:
- Examination of the right knee: Significant effusion present. Slight ecchymosis. Pain to palpation of the medial joint line. Lachman test positive. Decreased range of motion. Active and passive range of motion is decreased. Pain to flexion and extension. Muscle strength 5/5 bilaterally.

RESULTS

X-ray of the right knee was obtained and reviewed today. No fractures or abnormalities were noted.

ASSESSMENT

Right knee pain, most consistent with an anterior cruciate ligament tear.

PLAN

At this point, I have discussed the diagnosis, treatment options, and the need for further evaluation with the patient. We have discussed the potential need for surgical intervention. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to further evaluate the integrity of the anterior cruciate ligament. We will re-evaluate after we have the results of the MRI. We discussed the importance of ice and anti-inflammatory medications. I have recommended that the patient wear a knee immobilizer and use crutches when walking. I have also recommended that the patient limit his activities. He should limit his jogging activities until we have the results of his MRI.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me after the MRI results are obtained. }}<|eot_id|>
