summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Right heel pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Dennis Smith is a 34-year-old male who presents today for the evaluation of right heel pain. The onset of his pain began several months ago. The patient states that the pain is worse after activity and upon waking in the morning. He describes the pain as tightness and a tearing sensation. He states that the pain is alleviated with walking for about 10 to 15 minutes. The patient reports that he is training for a triathlon and he is concerned that his pain may be impacting his ability to focus on his training.

The patient reports that he runs 10 kilometers in the morning, bikes for 45 minutes to an hour at lunch, and swims for 30 minutes to an hour in the evening. He states that he trains 5 days a week, alternating between long runs and long bike rides. He also swims on the other 5 days of the week. He denies stretching. He reports that he jumps on his bike and starts slow and then picks up speed as he warms up.

The patient states that he does not take any medication for his pain. He reports that he has not had any significant relief from his pain.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports right heel pain.

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK
- Examination of the right leg: The Thompson test is negative. Pain with palpation of the right Achilles tendon. Pain with dorsiflexion of the right ankle.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

Achilles tendinitis.

Patient presents today for the evaluation of right heel pain. The onset of his pain began several months ago. The patient states that the pain is worse after activity and upon waking in the morning. He describes the pain as tightness and a tearing sensation. He states that the pain is alleviated with walking for about 10 to 15 minutes. The patient reports that he is training for a triathlon and he is concerned that his pain may be impacting his ability to focus on his training. He reports that he runs 10 kilometers in the morning, bikes for 45 minutes to an hour at lunch, and swims for 30 minutes to an hour in the evening. He states that he trains 5 days a week, alternating between long runs and long bike rides. He also swims on the other 5 days of the week. He denies stretching. He reports that he jumps on his bike and starts slow and then picks up speed as he warms up. He denies any medication use for his pain. He reports that he has not had any significant relief from his pain.

After reviewing his examination today, I have found that the patient has achilles tendinitis. I have recommended that he stop training for a week or two. I have recommended that he take ibuprofen or advil, 3 tablets every 6 hours, as an anti-inflammatory. I have also recommended that he see physical therapy to work on strength and stretching. I have ordered a couple of physical therapy visits. I have recommended that he follow up in 7 to 10 days after his first couple of physical therapy appointments. }}<|eot_id|>
