summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Bilateral knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Sophia Johnson is a pleasant 34-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for evaluation of bilateral knee pain. The onset of her pain began approximately 6 months ago. She denies any specific trauma that caused the pain. The patient describes the pain as a deep, achy pain that is felt behind the kneecaps. She also reports hearing creaking in her knees. The pain is exacerbated by activities such as getting up from a seated position and going up and down stairs. She states that the pain improves with rest. She has previously tried ice and Ace wraps on her knees. She also takes ibuprofen and Aleve, although she tries to avoid medications.

The patient states that she enjoys running and has run 5 to 6 miles per day. However, she has decreased her mileage to less than half a mile due to knee pain. She prefers to run outdoors.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain. Denies swelling or redness in the knees. Denies history of knee injuries.

PHYSICAL EXAM

Musculoskeletal
- Examination of the lower extremities: Normal gait. Strength is 3/5 for abduction of the legs bilaterally. The remainder of muscle strength for the lower extremities is 5/5.
- Examination of the knees: No overlying erythema or ecchymosis. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Patient is able to squat but with pain. Lachman anterior and posterior drawer and McMurray tests are negative bilaterally.
- Neurological: Patella and achilles reflexes are symmetrical.

RESULTS

X-ray of both knees were obtained and reviewed today. These reveal no fractures or osteoarthritis.

ASSESSMENT AND PLAN

- Bilateral knee pain.
- Based on the patient's history and physical exam, her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome.
- At this time, I have recommended that the patient continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatory as needed to help with the pain.
- I have also recommended that the patient undergo physical therapy. The physical therapist will show her a number of lower extremity exercises to help increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics.

Patient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.
