summary is: {{ CHIEF COMPLAINT

Low back pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Kyle Smith is a pleasant 34-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of low back pain. He states that the onset of his pain began approximately 2 weeks ago. The patient reports that he was lifting a box of books when he felt a pop in his low back. He denies proper lifting technique. The patient describes his pain as achy and sharp in nature. He reports that the pain radiates down his left leg. He states that sitting is usually okay for him. He denies numbness, tingling, or weakness. He denies any loss of bladder or bowel control. He reports that he has tried ibuprofen and Tylenol which sometimes helps. He denies that anyone has discussed the option of surgery with him. He states that he is an Olympic weightlifter with books. He notes that he is inconsistent with his exercise and has a sedentary job. He denies tobacco use. He has a history of playing soccer.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports low back pain.
Neurological: Denies numbness, tingling, weakness, or loss of sensation.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 130/70 mmHg
Respiratory rate: 18

PHYSICAL EXAM

Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs, rubs, or gallops.
Musculoskeletal: No overlying erythema or bruising on the skin. Tenderness to palpation at the L4-5 disk space. Right-sided lumbar paravertebral tenderness. Pain with lumbar flexion. Positive straight leg raise test.

RESULTS

MRI of the lumbar spine revealed disk desiccation, disk bulge, and paracentral disk herniation resulting in moderate right neuroforaminal stenosis.

ASSESSMENT

Lumbar back pain at the L4-5 disk space due to a herniated disk with radiculopathy.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have explained to him that his symptoms are in line with a lumbar radiculopathy. I have recommended that we proceed with a lumbar epidural steroid injection with a strong NSAID called Methylprednisolone. This injection should help with the inflammation that is causing his pain. I have explained the risks associated with this procedure and he is in agreement. We will proceed with this injection today.

INSTRUCTIONS

We will proceed with the epidural steroid injection today. }}<|eot_id|>
