Left knee pain.

HISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS

Angela Johnson is a pleasant 48-year-old female who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of left knee pain. The onset of her pain began one week ago when she was playing with her niece. She states that she heard a pop in her knee at the time of the injury. The patient reports that she is unable to bear weight on her left leg and has been limping since the injury. She denies any recent trauma to the right knee. The patient states that she has been taking Tylenol for pain and has wrapped her knee with kinesiology tape. She denies the use of ibuprofen due to a recent ulcer. The patient rates her pain as 4 out of 10. She states that her pain is constant and throbbing in nature. The patient reports that she has been unable to run with her niece. She notes that she has been taking Tylenol every 4 hours as needed for pain.

MEDICAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she has a history of a recent ulcer.

SOCIAL HISTORY

The patient reports that she enjoys running.

MEDICATIONS

The patient reports that she takes Tylenol.

REVIEW OF SYSTEMS

Musculoskeletal: Reports left knee pain.

VITALS

Blood pressure: 118/76 mmHg
Heart rate: 72 bpm
Respiratory rate: 16
Temperature: 98.6 degrees F

PHYSICAL EXAM

MSK: Examination of the left knee: Edema present, effusion present, moderate ROM, pain with flexion and extension. Negative Lachman's test, negative valgus and varus test.

RESULTS

X-ray of the left knee was obtained and is reviewed in the office and is normal. There are no fractures or bony abnormalities.

ASSESSMENT

Left knee pain.

PLAN

After reviewing the patient's examination and radiographic findings, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her symptoms are consistent with a MCL strain. I have recommended that we treat her conservatively with a course of tramadol 50 mg, 2 tablets twice a day as needed for pain. I have also recommended that she continue to take Tylenol as needed for breakthrough pain. Additionally, I have recommended that she wear a knee immobilizer for one week and ice her knee 3-4 times a day for 20-30 minutes at a time. I have also recommended that she refrain from running for the next 3 weeks. The patient understands and agrees with this treatment plan.

INSTRUCTIONS

The patient will follow up with me in 4 weeks if she has not improved. }}<|eot_id|>
